course: dictionary
lesson: 15714
title: Towny: town or country?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Towny is an informal word, the kind people use in chatty speech. It describes something that belongs to the town, or someone who likes town life. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning fits.
explain: «Lily is very towny. She has never seen a cow up close.» The clue is that she has never seen a cow. Someone who is towny is used to town things, like shops, buses and busy streets, not to animals in fields.
explain: Towny can also be a noun: a towny, or a townie. «The farm children laughed and called the visitors townies when their shoes got muddy.» Country people and students sometimes use it to describe people from the town. The laughing and the teasing show it is friendly, but it can sometimes be unkind.
explain: Check the clues, then swap in your meaning. If «towny» can be replaced by «from the town, not used to the countryside» and the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Towny means belonging to the town, or liking town life.
fact: Towny is informal, so you will hear it in chatty speech and stories more than in formal writing.
fact: A towny, also spelled townie, is a person from the town, as country people or students might see them.
fact: Towny can be friendly or teasing, and sometimes it is unkind, so the context matters.
fact: «Towny folk» means people who belong to the town and like town life.
exercise: «Lily is very towny. She has never seen a cow up close.» What does towny suggest about Lily? | She is a farmer who loves cows | She is a famous singer | She has lived on a farm all her life | *She is from the town and is not used to the countryside
exercise: The farm children laughed and called the visitors townies. What is a townie? | A cow that lives in a field | A farm worker who grows apples | A kind of tractor | *A person from the town, as country people might see them
exercise: Nadia says towny is a formal word, only for school essays. Is she right? | Yes, it is only used in law books | No, it is a word for a grey day | Yes, it means a king | *No, towny is informal, so you hear it in chatty speech
test: «The new boy was towny and did not know how to milk a goat.» What does towny mean here? | *Belonging to the town and used to town life | Brave and bold | Very tall and strong | Good at cooking
test: Which form is the comparative of towny? | townest | *townier | towner | towniest
exam: Which describes a towny person? | Someone who sings in a choir at night | Someone who lives in a tent on a mountain | *Someone who loves the busy high street and has never seen a field of sheep | Someone who only swims in the sea
